    "language": "en",
    "md5": "33bb0a21bbb5a1c69713ed0af42ddc6a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1566,
    "chunk_text": "The awareness session, jointly organised by the Digital Economy Ministry and SEC, under the theme \u2018Let\u2019s Together for a Safer Digital Future\u2019, focused on the lessons that could be drawn from the jurisdictions that have already established frameworks for virtual assets.\n\nParticular attention was paid to the compliance frameworks including the know your customer requirements, anti-money laundering controls and transaction monitoring systems, areas viewed as essential to any future regulatory regime.\n\nThe experts also highlighted the role of blockchain technology in improving transparency and traceability, noting that the technology itself could assist the regulators in monitoring the transactions and strengthening oversight.\n\nThe participants were further briefed on how virtual assets are created, traded and stored, as well as their growing use in investment products such as exchange-traded funds, self-custody holdings and international payment systems.\n\nThe focus was also on investor protection, corporate accountability and development of a regulatory framework suited to Sri Lanka\u2019s financial system and digital economy ambitions.\n\nSenior Advisor to President Dr. Hans Wijesuriya, Digital Economy Ministry Secretary Waruna Sri Dhanapala, Digital Economy Deputy Minister Eranga Weeraratne, SEC Chairman Senior Professor Hareendra Dissabandara, Financial Intelligence Unit Director Dr. Subhani Keerthirathna and senior officials from the Colombo Stock Exchange, Central Bank of Sri Lanka and Digital Economy Ministry were among those attending."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
